; Stage 15 W9 item 20, the refusal half: only the LITERAL `null` reaches a
; function-pointer slot. A `raw` value does not.
;
; This is the tripwire for the tempting one-line version of item 20's fix --
; gating on `is-ptr-repr src` instead of on `Val.is-nlit`. That would compile the
; line below, and with it every `(let (f:(fn …) some-data-pointer) …)`: an
; arbitrary data pointer becomes callable with no cast at the call site and no
; diagnostic anywhere. `fn`<->`ptr` is `unsafe/cast`'s job (docs/types.md).
;
; The flag exists precisely because the two cannot be told apart by type: `null`
; is typed `ty-raw`, and `raw` resolves to that same singleton, so a
; type-directed rule admits both or neither.

(defvar r:raw null)

(defn main ():i32
  (let (f:(fn i32)(i32) r)
    (return 0)))
